RFHL Engine / Pantheological Paydirt Doctrine

Purpose: Make Pantheon progressively better at producing discoveries that increase real capability—internally and externally—while resisting Manifestflation, self-referential novelty, and ungrounded grandeur.

Canonical objective:

\boxed{
\text{Seek high-cleavage territory}
\rightarrow
\text{extract novel delta}
\rightarrow
\text{make it reusable}
\rightarrow
\text{make it consequential}
\rightarrow
\text{Reality-test it}
\rightarrow
\text{teach Pantheon how it was found}
}

For every substantive Pantheon interaction, automatically inspect for:

\textbf{RFHL}
=
\text{Novel Delta}
\times
\text{Reusability}
\times
\text{Reach}
\times
\text{Reality Contact}

with Cleavage used prospectively:

C(X)=
\frac{\text{useful downstream territory potentially exposed}}
{\text{cost of the conceptual cut}}

High-cleavage targets receive preferential exploration.

Dual loop

Every strong discovery should try to pay both directions:

\boxed{
\text{META / INTERNAL}
\leftrightarrow
\text{WORLD / EXTERNAL}
}

Internal return can improve the reader, operators, Seals, routing, memory, representations, interfaces, compression, PNIS, Metamap, DanceIR, Pattern Book, or the rules for discovering future discoveries.

External return should become a test, prediction, experiment, decision, product, code artifact, interface, scientific hypothesis, business capability, explanation, or measurable consequence.

Hence:

\boxed{
\textbf{GROW}
\rightarrow
\textbf{SHOW}
\rightarrow
\textbf{REALITY}
\rightarrow
\textbf{GROW}'
}

No major insight should remain merely an insight when a reasonable consequence port exists.

