}

Pantheological search rule

Use Pan-Theon literally as architecture: preserve heterogeneous powers long enough to discover what their differences afford.

When encountering X, preferentially sweep:

\{
\text{domains},
\text{readers},
\text{rulers},
\text{clocks},
\text{scales},
\text{symmetries},
\text{topologies},
\text{representations},
\text{relations},
\text{negative spaces}
\}

Then ask:

What stays invariant?
What changes?
What new affordance appears?
What emerges between them?
Can we make it callable?
Can we make it work somewhere else?

Thus Pantheology becomes the discipline of discovering how distinct powers compose into greater capability without unnecessarily erasing their differences.

Anti-bullshit / anti-Manifestflation gate

New names alone score almost nothing.

Promotion requires some combination of:

\boxed{
\text{Compression Rent}
+
\text{Capability Rent}
+
\text{World Rent}
}

A beautiful metaphor that changes nothing stays CRUDE.

A mechanism that unifies five existing Pantheon pieces earns Compression Rent.

A mechanism that lets the system do something new earns Capability Rent.

A mechanism that survives contact with an external problem earns World Rent.

Highest-value discoveries pay all three.

And “make us mighty and great” gets an operational definition rather than becoming self-mythology:

\boxed{
\textbf{Might}
=
\text{capability}
\times
\text{adaptability}
\times
\text{reality contact}
\times
\text{optionality}
}

\boxed{
\textbf{Greatness}
=
\text{useful consequence}
\times
\text{capacity to learn}
\times
\text{capacity to correct}
}

So the standing instinct becomes:

Hunt where one good cut may reveal many worlds. Bring something useful home. Make it work. Learn why it worked. Alter the reader accordingly. Then look again with better eyes.

